1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,833
Gold dust, sprinkled into rock samples.

2
00:00:03,067 --> 00:00:06,133
That's all it took to build a $6 billion company.

3
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:13,700
In 1993, a tiny Calgary miner called Bre-X bought a jungle gold project in Borneo, Indonesia.

4
00:00:14,100 --> 00:00:18,000
By 1997, it claimed 71 million ounces.

5
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:21,900
The stock went from pennies to a $6 billion company.

6
00:00:22,167 --> 00:00:23,667
Pension funds piled in.

7
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:27,867
That March, a partner drilled its own test holes beside Bre-X's.

8
00:00:28,267 --> 00:00:32,767
On March 19th, chief geologist Michael de Guzman fell from a helicopter.

9
00:00:33,167 --> 00:00:36,800
A week later, the partner reported insignificant amounts of gold.

10
00:00:37,233 --> 00:00:43,167
On May 4th, independent auditors confirmed it: the core samples had been salted with outside gold.

11
00:00:43,567 --> 00:00:46,433
Two days later, the shares sank to 6 cents.

12
00:00:46,733 --> 00:00:49,767
About 40,000 shareholders were nearly wiped out.

13
00:00:50,167 --> 00:00:54,367
The Ontario Teachers' Pension Plan alone lost $100 million.

14
00:00:54,833 --> 00:00:57,000
To be fair, no one was ever convicted.

15
00:00:57,400 --> 00:01:05,133
Exploration chief John Felderhof sold $84 million of stock in 1996, and was found not guilty of insider trading.

16
00:01:05,533 --> 00:01:09,000
The lesson: a company's own results aren't proof.

17
00:01:09,233 --> 00:01:15,167
Since 2001, Canadian miners need a qualified expert's sign-off, and often an independent one.
